# An Arch MICROVM WITH NO DESKTOP: KERNEL, INITRAMFS, ROOT FILESYSTEM, SERIAL LOGIN.
#
# THE SAME SHAPE AS THE ALPINE, DEBIAN AND NIXOS MICROVMS: a kernel, an initramfs, and ONE FLAT ext4
# qcow2 with no partition table, booted with `-kernel` because a microvm has no firmware to read a
# boot sector.
# MEASURED: `virtio_blk` and `ext4` ARE BUILT IN (`modules.builtin` names both), so the initramfs
# carries only `virtio_mmio`. AND THE GUEST WAS BOOTED BEFORE THIS FILE WAS WRITTEN, which is how the
# `systemd-firstboot` prompt was found - see the third assertion.
# NOTHING HERE INSTALLS arch. `.github/arch-rootfs.sh` builds the three files; this walkthrough
# boots them and reads the console.
#
# THE ASSERTION IS THE SERIAL LINE, NOT A SCREEN. There is no framebuffer on this machine - a GPU
# needs PCIe, and `pcie=off` means there is none - so the recording is the "NO DISPLAY" card the
# recorder draws while a guest has no adapter.

walkthrough "arch microvm - a root filesystem, a serial login, and no PCIe"
  resolution 1024x768
  recording_resolution 1024x768

  # THE THREE FILES THE CI STEP BUILDS, named here so the boot step can refer to them.
  needs arch-vmlinuz arch-initramfs arch.qcow2

  # THE ROOT FILESYSTEM, BUILT BY THE CI STEP AND NOT INSTALLED BY THIS FILE.
  disk arch.qcow2

  # `without a display` AND NOT `with a console`: this guest has no VGA adapter at all, which is the
  # honest shape for a machine with no PCIe.
  #
  # `rootfstype=ext4` IS NAMED BECAUSE THE INITRAMFS NEEDS TO AGREE WITH THE IMAGE. `console=ttyS0`
  # is the only place this guest can be read.
  boot as a microvm from kernel "arch-vmlinuz" and initramfs "arch-initramfs" with "console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4" without a display with memory 1024, waiting up to 180s

  # THE FACTS THIS GUEST CAN SHOW, AND WHY THEY ARE NOT THE SAME AS ITS TWINS'.
  #
  # MEASURED IN CI on run 44683664921: this guest booted COMPLETELY - every target, sshd,
  # `archlinux login: root (automatic login)` - and the word "vda" NEVER APPEARED in its console
  # log. The kernel line that names the disk is printed before the initramfs hands over, and
  # systemd's own terminal setup redraws the screen from that point, so on this distribution the
  # early line is not in the captured log while it is on the others. Asserting "vda" here fails a
  # guest that is up.
  #
  # SO THE PROMPT IS THE ASSERTION. It cannot be reached without a root filesystem, which is the
  # same fact one step later.
  the console will say "Arch Linux"

  # AND IT IS THE LOGIN PROMPT, NOT A FIRST-BOOT QUESTION. MEASURED before `systemd-firstboot` was
  # masked, the console showed
  #
  #   Initial Setup
  #   Please enter the new timezone name or number ("list" to list options, empty to skip):
  #
  # on a machine that had mounted its disk and started systemd - so an assertion on "login" alone
  # would have passed on a guest nobody could log into. `archlinux login` names the hostname this
  # image is built with, so the question cannot satisfy it.
  the console will say "archlinux login"

  # TWENTY SECONDS OF A GUEST AT A PROMPT, so the recording is a machine that stayed up.
  wait 20s

  stop the machine by killing it, waiting up to 60s
